# Test projects a change makes pass that the baseline's run of failed - a
# program the compiler built and ran wrong, or a refusal whose report lacked
# what the project expects.  One per line, the project's directory name under
# tests/tests/projects, then the reason.  A program the baseline REFUSES and
# the change makes compile goes in tests/started-compiling instead.
#
# A line may also name a negative test file, `negative/<file>.cryo`, whose
# report the change alters (the baseline's census fails it), an example,
# `examples/<name>`, the baseline does not build, or a unit-test file by its
# path under tests/tests, `stdlib/<file>.cryo`, whose object the change moves
# (its object is left out of the comparison and must have moved; the baseline
# may fail the tests the file holds, and no others).
#
# `make verify ARGS="--baseline REV"` applies the lines this file carries and
# REV's copy does not.  It asserts that the baseline's test census fails on
# exactly the projects the two files declare, and the tree's census passing
# them is the rest of the claim.  Their objects are left out of the
# comparison: what the baseline compiled for them is the program being
# corrected.  A line stays once committed: against a later baseline it is
# inert, against an earlier one it is asserted again.  A later change that
# makes the same program start passing again rewrites its line's reason,
# which declares it against a baseline holding the old reason.
